== Artificial-intelligence scope audit ==

[[Artificial intelligence]] remains a field-level concept rather than a synonym for machine learning or generative AI.

It includes symbolic, statistical, search, planning, knowledge-representation, language, robotic, and hybrid approaches.

No claim is made that task competence proves human-like cognition or consciousness.

== Intelligent-agent audit ==

[[Intelligent agent]] remains the general agent abstraction:

<pre>
perceive
→ select action
→ act
</pre>

Rationality is defined relative to objectives, available percepts/knowledge, and actions rather than as omniscience.

The page explicitly allows non-LLM, non-language, non-learning agents.

== AI-agent audit ==

[[AI agent]] is modeled as a compound system rather than a model label.

The Core/Technical pages preserve:

<pre>
model
+ controller/orchestration
+ actions/tools
+ state/memory
+ feedback
(+ optional planning/monitoring)
→ agent system
</pre>

No component is asserted to be universally mandatory.

NIST's current Agentic AI work is used as evidence that the terminology has institutional relevance, not as the sole definition.

== Planning audit ==

[[Planning (artificial intelligence)]] preserves the established automated-planning field.

The article distinguishes:

<pre>
planning ≠ search by definition
planning ≠ execution
planning ≠ textual chain-of-thought
</pre>

Ghallab/Nau/Traverso planning-and-acting literature anchors the need for monitoring, replanning, and continual deliberation during real execution.

Modern LLM task decomposition is treated as one implementation/application layer rather than a redefinition of planning.

== Tool-use audit ==

[[Tool use (artificial intelligence)]] covers selection, argument construction, invocation, feedback, interpretation, and multi-tool orchestration.

The page rejects:

<pre>
tool use = function calling
</pre>

as too narrow.

A single tool call also does not establish agenthood.

== Agent-memory audit ==

[[Agent memory (artificial intelligence)]] is an architecture-level persistence/recall capability.

The corpus explicitly separates memory from:

* current context;
* vector-database storage;
* RAG;
* biological memory.

Human-memory labels such as episodic/semantic/working memory are treated as analogies/taxonomies, not proof of biological equivalence.

Because current 2025–2026 literature still contains competing taxonomies, the concept is assigned '''Supported'''.

== Multi-agent-system audit ==

[[Multi-agent system]] is anchored in the pre-LLM distributed-AI literature.

The page preserves:

<pre>
multiple model calls ≠ multi-agent system by definition
multi-agent system ≠ distributed system
multi-agent system ≠ distributed cognition
</pre>

Modern LLM-agent teams are treated as an application of a much older field.

== Autonomous-agent audit ==

[[Autonomous agent (artificial intelligence)]] treats autonomy as graded operational control.

An agent can be autonomous within fixed:

* goals;
* permissions;
* tools;
* budgets;
* sandboxes;
* approval boundaries.

The page explicitly rejects equating autonomy with absence of constraints or with unsupervised learning.

== Large-language-model audit ==

[[Large language model]] is kept at the model layer.

It is not equated with a chatbot or an agent.

The page preserves:

<pre>
LLM              → large-scale language model
foundation model → broad pretrained adaptable model
Transformer      → architecture family
chatbot          → application/interface
AI agent         → compound action system
</pre>

No universal parameter-count threshold for “large” is asserted.

Transformer architecture is described as dominant in contemporary LLMs rather than definitional.

== Cross-wave audit ==

Wave 8 connects cleanly to earlier waves:

* [[Machine learning]] remains a technique/field inside broader AI.
* [[Reasoning]] remains broader than formal logic and is not replaced by LLM generation.
* [[Memory]] / [[Working memory]] remain cognitive concepts, not agent-storage components.
* [[Distributed system]] remains distinct from [[Multi-agent system]].
* [[Distributed cognition]] remains distinct from computational multi-agent architecture.
* [[Human-computer interaction]] remains a human/system field rather than an agent interface implementation.
* [[Language]], [[Syntax]], [[Semantics]], and [[Distributional semantics]] remain linguistic/representational concepts rather than LLM-specific constructs.
* [[Computer network]] and [[Program (computing)]] provide infrastructure/tool context without being redefined as agent components.

No downstream Wave 8 page requires changing an earlier canonical definition.

